<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vindhyashakti</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">3rd century</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vindhyashakti was the founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vakataka-dynasty">Vakataka dynasty</a>, which rose in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in the mid-3rd century as the Satavahana dynasty broke up. He is known from a Vakataka inscription at Ajanta and from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puranas">Puranas</a>, which name him as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a> chief. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pravarasena-i">Pravarasena I</a> turned the family's domain into a large kingdom across central India.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Vindhyashakti is thought to have ruled from about 250 to 275. His name may come from the goddess Vindhyavasini, or it may be a title or biruda referring to his family's lands in the Vindhya mountains rather than a personal name. Scholars have placed his original home in the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a>, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madhya-pradesh">Madhya Pradesh</a> and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a>; K. P. Jayaswal took Bagat, a village in the Jhansi district, to be the home of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vakataka-dynasty">Vakatakas</a>.</p><p>V. V. Mirashi pointed out that the name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vakataka-dynasty">Vakataka</a> first occurs on a pillar fragment at Amaravati, which records the gift of a householder named Vakataka and his two wives. There is no evidence, however, that this man's name had anything to do with the dynasty, or that he came from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a>, since he was visiting the Buddhist establishment at Amaravati as a pilgrim.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>No inscription from his own reign has been found. The much later inscription in Ajanta Cave XVI, of the time of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harishena">Harishena</a>, calls him the banner of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vakataka-dynasty">Vakataka</a> family and a dvija, or twice-born, says that he added to his power by fighting great battles and had a large cavalry, and compares his majesty to that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indra">Indra</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a>. It prefixes no royal title to his name.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puranas">Puranas</a>, whose historical reliability is questionable, make him a ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vidisha">Vidisha</a> in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madhya-pradesh">Madhya Pradesh</a>, which is not considered correct. The Vayu Purana gives him an implausibly long reign of 96 years, and the Puranic texts place Naga kings between his time and that of his son Pravira.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Vindhyashakti seems to have been largely forgotten or ignored after his death, perhaps overshadowed by his son and successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pravarasena-i">Pravarasena I</a>. All the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>-plate grants of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vakataka-dynasty">Vakataka dynasty</a> begin the family's genealogy with Pravarasena I rather than with him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Vindhyashakti” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The dynasty was founded in the mid-3rd century by <b>Vindhyashakti</b> in the area of Vidarbha, as the Satavahana dynasty broke up.</blockquote><a href="/state/vakataka-dynasty">Vakataka dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
